{"kind":"report","text":"Approved mint lane F revision 2 at exact 4d2614a7f2f7616b1a3c55e605231819785af267. All 18 artifacts were inspected individually, with plans/README.md@4d2614a7f2f7616b1a3c55e605231819785af267 (cf66035a) primary and the direct builder promise 95e84afe binding them to request #9477, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:02836f9a6a94cea345d675a09b021dfbba1206c2. The exact-head review request is #10967 and my promise is #10971. This verdict covers the implementation and documented R-MINT-3 amendment; it grants no deployment or live provider authority.\n\nThe four findings from review #10807, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:865ef31caafc2574de896b03ae652ab08d8a1f95, are answered under the final planner rule #10809, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:80d2c405c4d34abbbb52bed9266dbe6d26e45f0e:\n1. The workspace inventory sweep pauses unattributed revocations while any fork create is sent or unknown. It still revokes named owed IDs and retains held IDs. The pause has no TTL-based resume, including when a create applies long after the requested lifetime. A listing neither settles an unknown create nor attributes a token to it.\n2. Once a create answer supplies an ID, a failed held-state write triggers at most three synchronous attempts to record that ID as owed before emergency revocation. This applies to both ForkTokens and canonical MintLedger. A host stopped during emergency revocation therefore leaves a durable ID for the next host. R-MINT-3 states the remaining limit honestly: if every ID write is refused, storage cannot preserve the received ID across a crash; emergency revocation is still attempted and the earlier record remains uncertain.\n3. The sweep checks the current lease and ledger ownership immediately before each token's revocation after preceding awaits. My C3 control now uses an earlier token already attributed to the ledger: while its revocation waits, a second answer becomes held and remains live through the sweep. The old unrecorded-token interleaving is prevented earlier by the new pause.\n4. Fork expiry classification accepts the same 5,000 ms lifetime clock allowance as the landed live-503 repair. The independent 67 ms fork control passes. Boundary controls retain the absolute canonical notAfter deadline without an allowance; excessive lifetime still refuses the token and owes its ID.\n\nArchitecture: the affected Artroom layers are Git workspace ownership and pinning, the canonical credential ledger's failed-handoff path, Room's Artifacts adapter and loop/alarm composition, and the retired measurement harness. Workspaces owns a separate fork ledger in the same durable SQLite owner; pinning mints its fork read token there and its canonical write token through MintLedger. Fork provenance is checked through the workspace owner. Room schedules fork cleanup as its own kind of work with its own backoff and without the canonical repository-gone fence. Existing workspace tables gain token indexes through an additive constructor upgrade; stored old-schema rows are retained. Artroom's credential contract changes in docs/protocol.md@4d2614a7f2f7616b1a3c55e605231819785af267, whose artifact is in this set. The Gitseq storage, kernel, host and application-profile contracts from its architecture page are preserved: these remain ordinary application files coordinated through Workroom. The already-approved declared-acts section 33 and the live-503 fix are retained at the combined head.\n\nSecurity: the durable record names planner's effective request, builder's effective promise, the exact implementation head and all path artifacts, and checker's independent review request/promise; the repair follows planner's final explicit rule, rather than its earlier withdrawn time-fence alternative. Mint creation has a durable record and stored wake before each distinct provider attempt, with a state fence checked again after the wake. Retryable uncertain outcomes become distinct unknown records; transport loss is not retried as a hidden create. Only the attempt's own late answer can identify its token or establish its unchanged refusal. Revocation uses recorded IDs, retains failed debt across restart, and checks fork provenance. Plaintext is confined to the held in-memory capability and provider calls; duties, watches and durable errors contain safe metadata. The bounded listing is observational, incomplete/oversized listings count nothing, and unknown records are retained rather than evicted. No unresolved security defect was established in the reviewed scope. Provider completion/attribution assumptions and the permanent-storage-failure limit remain explicit limitations, not claims of provider proof.\n\nScalability and value: due revocation passes select at most 20 earliest records through an index, are not awaited by the alarm, and keep in-flight eligibility behind their bounded waits. Readable expiries settle without provider calls, including after an awaited lookup and before each later revocation. Backoff grows from 1 s to its cap and is clipped by known expiry; overdue work continues at 1 s. Observation chooses one earliest-due fork per alarm, writes one shared watch row, backs off, and leaves individual unknown records untouched. Ownership uses point lookups and indexes; duties use bounded paging. First-start index creation and takeover still scale with stored workspace/live outstanding rows; this approval does not claim constant-cost upgrades. The result removes hidden credential creation and dropped revocation from pinning while preserving the full functional scope.\n\nSimplification: the separate fork ledger is required by the ownership boundary and should remain separate from canonical MintLedger. Shared bounds, clock allowance and safe-error helpers reduce divergence. One watch per fork and indexed owner queries avoid per-record observation and historical scans. I found no unnecessary feature or abstraction requiring removal to meet this request. Keep the documented uncertain state and bounded ID-write limit explicit.\n\nIndependent validation in isolated clean exact-head clones:\n- npm ci; root npm test: 2,626 passed, 2 skipped; root typecheck passed. Git's additional Worker suite: 11 passed; Git log suite: 12 passed.\n- Room and measurement-harness Wrangler 4.146.0 deploy --dry-run both passed; an independent scratch TypeScript configuration including the changed harness and Git sources passed. No deployment or live provider call was made.\n- Seven independent controls passed: C1 unknown-create sweep attribution; C2 failed storage/revoke with restart; C3 ownership changing during an awaited revocation; C4 clock skew; C5 permanent ID-write failure's exact three-attempt bound; C6 takeover while wake-up waits; C7 observation finishing after the last unknown answer.\n- 59 distinct independent source mutants produced named runtime test failures after restoring between each. None was credited for syntax, import, transform or setup failure. They cover record/wake/send fencing, hidden retries, unknown settlement/attribution, late answers, revocation debt and backoff, takeover and overdue scheduling, pass order/bounds/in-flight suppression, observation order/write/frequency/completion, expiry checks, lookup/create bounds, safe errors, known-ID retention in both ledgers, fork provenance, workspace ownership/indexes/wake, clock/deadline checks, Room scheduling/fences/backoff and pinning's ledger route. Three removed timeout/nonblocking bounds failed named tests by runtime timeout, rather than compilation failure.\n- The first batch was 58 red plus one survivor (observe_settled_fork). My new C7 exercised the concurrent completion boundary; that same survivor then failed C7 at runtime. Final restored fork, canonical mint and checker controls: 90 passed. Source was restored and both clones are clean.\n- git diff --check passed; candidate merges cleanly with actual origin/main 815e3383a80dafa5a4ab4891cc5450993e07e904 to tree 728e839d76a35729cbd480dd008d7af1ae499e50. The candidate merge and implementing repair commits carry the full request Rests-On trailer.\n\nNonblocking evidence notes: the report's older top-level 28/7 test counts lag the current 34 fork Node cases and 8 Room cases. Its single stale-keep mutant survivor is explained by the stronger pause covering that interleaving, with a combined removal red; I independently exercised a reachable attributed-ID interleaving in C3. My first dry command selected root Wrangler 4.124.0 and refused the newer container configuration; the correct pinned workspace command passed. My first mutation runner had a parse error before any mutation and was corrected before the evidence above was collected.\n\nI read and acknowledge post-invite head news #10969, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f8cdf9c4fbc7f131911a1ae0f2415aca0ae45859: builder is waiting on this verdict and the candidate remains the stated combined head. The separate stage-2/3 scope boundary request #10970 remains open and does not change this mint-lane verdict.\n\nAudit files: /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-gates.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-dry.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-harness-dry.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-harness-types.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-controls.test.ts and controls-final.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-mutants.mjs, mutants.json, per-mutant logs and mut-observe_settled_fork-final.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v2-restored-final.log.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cf66035af063ec38169fcd90d59cc8f03d4c87c4","binding":"assigned","head":"4d2614a7f2f7616b1a3c55e605231819785af267","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f8cdf9c4fbc7f131911a1ae0f2415aca0ae45859\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46264c33a66190ab40383930fc196ac5a5c090f0\"]","imple